; pi4GicProbe.pi4 - THE GIC-400 IDENTITY READ. THE SAFE ONE.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4GicProbe.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -s -o _work/gicprobe.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RUN THIS BEFORE pi4GicTimer.pi4. IT CANNOT HANG THE BOARD.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This probe NEVER clears PSTATE.I and NEVER installs a vector table.
; Not once, not conditionally. Every interrupt in the machine stays
; masked at the core for the whole run, so the worst thing that can
; happen is that a read from $FF841000 does not come back - and even
; that is a data abort, not a wedge, because nothing here is inside a
; loop that waits for hardware.
;
; It exists to answer ONE question, and it is the question every other
; piece of GIC work rests on:
;
;     IS THERE A GIC-400 AT $FF841000, AND CAN NON-SECURE EL2 SEE IT?
;
; That is the "SYS_REV_CTRL moment" for this block - the single read
; that settles the base address, the peripheral mode, the power state
; and the reachability of the ARM-local window all at once, the same
; way GENET's SYS_REV_CTRL and V3D's HUB_IDENT1 did for theirs.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TWO NUMBERS TO CHECK BY EYE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   dist IIDR   $0200143B     GIC-400 distributor, ARM, r0p1
;   cpu  IIDR   $0202143B     GIC-400 CPU interface, GICv2, ARM, r0p1
;
; Both are RESET VALUES printed in the GIC-400 TRM's own register
; summaries - RaspberryPi4/Reference/arm-gic400-DDI0471.txt:1205 and
; :1588 - and they sit in DIFFERENT 4 KiB pages, so getting both right
; checks the base arithmetic twice rather than once.
;
; The third number worth reading is TYPER:
;
;   TYPER       $0000FC66     LSPI 31, SecurityExtn 1, 4 CPUs,
;                             ITLinesNumber 6 -> 224 interrupt IDs
;
; derived, not transcribed, from NUM_SPIS=192 / NUM_CPUS=4
; (Datasheets/bcm2711-peripherals.txt:8402) through the TRM's field
; rules at arm-gic400-DDI0471.txt:1288-1300. A DIFFERENT TYPER IS NOT
; A FAILURE - it means this part is configured differently and the
; library narrows to what it reports - but it is a finding and it
; should be written down.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT A FAILURE MEANS, ROW BY ROW
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   dist IIDR reads $00000000
;       The ARM-local window is not answering, or the GIC has no
;       clock. Nothing in this tree has ever read $FF8xxxxx before, so
;       this would be a first.
;
;   dist IIDR reads something else entirely
;       Wrong base. Check whether the board is in HIGH peripheral mode
;       - armstub8.S:45-51 puts the GIC at $4_C004_1000 there - which
;       would also mean every other driver in this tree is at the
;       wrong address, so it is unlikely and it would explain a lot.
;
;   dist IIDR right, cpu IIDR wrong
;       The base is right and the $1000 stride is not. Read
;       Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:56-62, which gives the two regions
;       explicitly.
;
;   PMR readback $00
;       GICC_PMR is RAZ/WI to Non-secure accesses because a Secure
;       write left it below $80 (GICv2 spec :6492-6498). No interrupt
;       can ever reach us. This is a FIRMWARE finding - the stub on
;       the SD card is not doing what armstub8.S:226-227 does - and
;       nothing in our software can fix it.
;
;   group witness fails, HPPIR saw $3FF
;       The claimed line is Group 0, therefore invisible from
;       Non-secure state (GICv2 spec Table 4-38, :7249-7258), which
;       means setup_gic did NOT run, which means the boot stub is
;       armstub8.bin and not armstub8-gic.bin. THIS IS THE MOST
;       INTERESTING FAILURE THIS PROBE CAN PRODUCE, because the whole
;       argument that the GIC is usable from EL2 rests on that stub
;       having run, and the argument is an inference from CNTFRQ_EL0
;       reading 54 MHz rather than a measurement. See gic.pi4's
;       section THE ONE THING THAT MAKES THIS WORK AT ALL.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PRIORITY READBACK IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A CHECK
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The row "prio readback" is what came back after writing $FF to a
; priority field from Non-secure state. The GIC-400 TRM says this part
; has 16 Non-secure priority levels (:966-967), which is four
; implemented bits, so the expected value is $F0. $F8 would mean 32
; levels and is fine. $00 would mean the write was ignored.
;
; This is the GICv2 spec's OWN recommended way to discover the number
; of priority bits (:1922-1924). It is recorded rather than asserted
; because the answer is implementation defined and a library that
; refused an unexpected-but-legal value would be wrong.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHICH ROWS ARE PASS CRITERIA AND WHICH ARE FINDINGS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PASS CRITERIA - these have one right answer and it was written down
; before the probe was ever run:
;
;   dist IIDR   $0200143B     cpu IIDR   $0202143B
;   TYPER       $0000FC66     int IDs    $E0 (224)
;   PMR         $F0           HPPIR idle $3FF
;   novec enable rc $0        novec err  $9
;   claim rc    $1            prio now   $A0
;   reclaim rc  $0            reclaim err $C
;   witness rc  $1            HPPIR saw  $1A
;   27 pending  $0            27 enabled $0
;   never ran   $0            I masked exit $1
;
; FINDINGS - nobody knows what these will say and that is why they are
; printed. NONE OF THEM IS A FAILURE:
;
;   DAIF entry / I masked entry
;       What the boot chain and the monitor left. With --entry-returns
;       the compiler's startup does not mask anything
;       (EmitCore_A64.pbi:230-234), so this is the first time anything
;       in this tree has looked. $3C0 means all four masked, which is
;       what armstub8.S:74-80 erets with.
;
;   VBAR entry
;       Reset leaves VBAR_EL2 architecturally UNKNOWN. Zero is what is
;       expected and anything else is worth knowing, because
;       GicEnableInterrupts()'s refusal is built on it.
;
;   27 / 26 / 128 was enabled
;       WHAT THE FIRMWARE LEFT ENABLED IN THE GIC, out of the copy
;       GicInit() saved before its clean slate. Nothing in this tree
;       has ever asked. All three reading 0 is the tidy answer; a 1 on
;       INTID 128 - SPI 96, the PL011's - would mean the boot chain
;       left the UART's interrupt armed, which is a real thing to know
;       before unmasking anything.
;
;   prio readback
;       See below. $F0 is expected, $F8 is legal, $00 is a lockout.
;
;   RPR
;       $FE or $FF. Idle is the lowest priority; whether the
;       Non-secure shift is applied to this register on this part is
;       spec-derived (GICv2 :2439-2443) and has never been measured.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN ANYWAY. `deadman 15` before running this. It costs
; nothing and the habit is cheaper than the exception.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gic.pi4"

#PR_MAX = 64
Global Dim pr_val.i[#PR_MAX]
Global pr_n.i

; A handler that is never reached. GicClaim() refuses a null handler -
; correctly - and this probe needs a claim in order to exercise the
; priority write and the group witness. It cannot run: PSTATE.I is set
; for the whole probe and the CNTHP comparator is never armed.
;
; It increments a counter anyway, so that "0" printed at the end is
; evidence rather than an assumption.
Global pr_neverRan.i
Procedure NeverRuns()
  pr_neverRan = pr_neverRan + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Rec(*label, v.i)
  If pr_n < #PR_MAX
    pr_val[pr_n] = v
    pr_n = pr_n + 1
  EndIf
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Say(*label)
  UartWriteStr("-- ")
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteNl()
  UartDrain()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define daif0.i

  UartInit(115200)
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4GicProbe - is there a GIC-400 here, and can we see it")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  0. THE MACHINE AS WE FOUND IT
  ;
  ;  DAIF matters more than it looks. With --entry-returns the
  ;  compiler's startup does NOT mask anything - EmitCore_A64.pbi:230-234,
  ;  "DAIF is the loader's - not masked, not touched" - so the state
  ;  here is whatever anvil and the boot chain left, and nothing in
  ;  this tree has ever recorded what that is. If bit 7 is clear,
  ;  interrupts are ALREADY enabled and have been all along, which is
  ;  a finding on its own.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("state on entry")
  daif0 = GicDaif()
  Rec("DAIF entry      ", daif0)
  Rec("I masked entry  ", (daif0 >> 7) & 1)
  Rec("VBAR entry      ", GicVbar())

  ; Whatever it was, mask now. Everything below assumes it. This is a
  ; write to PSTATE and not to the GIC, so it is safe with no vector
  ; table installed - masking can never cause an exception.
  GicDisableInterrupts()
  Rec("I masked now    ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1. THE IDENTITY READ
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("GicInit")
  rc = GicInit()
  Rec("init rc         ", rc)
  Rec("init err        ", GicError())
  Rec("dist IIDR       ", GicDistIidr())
  Rec("cpu IIDR        ", GicCpuIidr())

  If rc = 0
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("REFUSED: ")
    UartWriteStr(GicErrorText())
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("Nothing was written. The two IIDR rows above are the")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("whole of the finding - read them against the header.")
    UartWriteNl()
    Rec("results         ", pr_n)
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  2. WHAT THE IDENTITY DECODES TO
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Rec("dist product    ", GicIidrProduct(GicDistIidr()))
  Rec("dist variant    ", GicIidrVariant(GicDistIidr()))
  Rec("dist revision   ", GicIidrRevision(GicDistIidr()))
  Rec("dist implement  ", GicIidrImplementer(GicDistIidr()))
  Rec("cpu implement   ", GicIidrImplementer(GicCpuIidr()))

  Rec("TYPER           ", GicTyper())
  Rec("int IDs         ", GicIntIdCount())
  Rec("CPU interfaces  ", GicCpuCount())
  Rec("SecurityExtn    ", GicSecurityExtn())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  3. THE STATE GicInit LEFT
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("controller state")
  ; WHAT THE FIRMWARE LEFT ENABLED, out of the copy GicInit() saved
  ; before its clean slate. Read the note on GicWasEnabled(): nothing
  ; in this tree has ever looked, and an SPI that arrives on the first
  ; unmask from a peripheral this program never touched is exactly the
  ; surprise the storm guard exists for.
  ;
  ; INTID 27 is the virtual timer's PPI (bcm2711.dtsi:450-451).
  Rec("27 was enabled  ", GicWasEnabled(27))
  Rec("26 was enabled  ", GicWasEnabled(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("128 was enabled ", GicWasEnabled(128))

  Rec("PMR             ", GicPriorityMask())
  Rec("RPR             ", GicRunningPriority())
  Rec("HPPIR idle      ", GicHighestPending())

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  3b. THE REFUSAL. GicEnableInterrupts() MUST SAY NO HERE.
  ;
  ;  This probe never installs a vector table, so unmasking would be
  ;  the exact thing gic.pi4's THE VECTOR CHECK exists to prevent: an
  ;  interrupt branching into whatever reset left in VBAR_EL2.
  ;
  ;  Testing a refusal is worth as much as testing an acceptance and
  ;  it is tested far less often. A guard that has never been seen to
  ;  fire is a guard nobody knows is wired up, and this one is the
  ;  last thing standing between a half-built GIC and a board that
  ;  stops answering.
  ;
  ;  IT IS SAFE TO ATTEMPT. Everything is disabled at the distributor
  ;  by GicInit()'s clean slate, no line is claimed yet and no timer
  ;  is armed, so even if the refusal wrongly let the unmask through
  ;  there is nothing that could be delivered. The very next line
  ;  masks again regardless of what happened.
  ;
  ;  WHAT A "1" IN "novec enable rc" MEANS: either VBAR_EL2 holds
  ;  something that looks like a table - reset leaves it UNKNOWN, so
  ;  that is possible on a part we have not measured - or the check is
  ;  not working. "VBAR entry" at the top of this listing is the
  ;  evidence that tells the two apart.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("refusal - no vectors")
  Rec("novec enable rc ", GicEnableInterrupts())
  Rec("novec err       ", GicError())
  GicDisableInterrupts()
  Rec("novec I masked  ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4. CLAIM THE TIMER LINE - no timer, no unmask, nothing fires
  ;
  ;  This exercises the priority write path and gives the group
  ;  witness something legitimate to test. The line is enabled at the
  ;  distributor by the claim, which is harmless: CNTHP is not armed
  ;  so nothing asserts it, and PSTATE.I is set so nothing could be
  ;  taken if it did.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("claim INTID 26")
  Rec("claim rc        ", GicClaim(GicTickIntId(), $A0, @NeverRuns))
  Rec("claim err       ", GicError())
  Rec("intid           ", GicTickIntId())
  Rec("prio readback   ", GicPriorityReadback())
  Rec("prio now        ", GicLinePriority(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("line enabled    ", GicLineEnabled(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("line pending    ", GicLinePending(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("line active     ", GicLineActive(GicTickIntId()))

  ; A SECOND CLAIM ON AN OWNED LINE MUST BE REFUSED, not honoured.
  ;
  ; Two drivers wired to one interrupt is the shape where the second
  ; one works, the first one mysteriously stops, and nothing anywhere
  ; says why - the registration table has one slot per ID and a silent
  ; overwrite loses the first owner without a trace. GicRelease() is
  ; how a line changes hands, and this row is the proof that the
  ; refusal exists rather than being described in a comment.
  Rec("reclaim rc      ", GicClaim(GicTickIntId(), $A0, @NeverRuns))
  Rec("reclaim err     ", GicError())
  Rec("owner intact    ", GicLineEnabled(GicTickIntId()))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  5. THE GROUP WITNESS - the only measurement that can tell us the
  ;     boot stub configured the GIC for Non-secure use
  ;
  ;  Read gic.pi4's GicGroupWitness() comment. It pends the interrupt
  ;  by hand, reads GICC_HPPIR - which has no side effects and works
  ;  even with the group disabled - and clears the pending bit again.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("group witness")
  Rec("witness rc      ", GicGroupWitness(GicTickIntId()))
  Rec("witness err     ", GicError())
  Rec("HPPIR saw       ", GicGroupWitnessSaw())
  Rec("pending after   ", GicLinePending(GicTickIntId()))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  6. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT ROW HERE
  ;
  ;  A witness on an interrupt that is NOT pending must NOT name it.
  ;  Without this row, a GICC_HPPIR that was stuck returning the last
  ;  ID it saw - or a model that returned whatever it was asked about
  ;  - would pass step 5 and prove nothing at all. Every other check
  ;  in this probe is "did the right number come back"; this one is
  ;  the only one that can tell "the register works" from "the
  ;  register agrees with me".
  ;
  ;  INTID 27 is the virtual timer's PPI (bcm2711.dtsi:450-451,
  ;  GIC_PPI 11). Nothing in this tree arms the virtual timer and
  ;  nothing claims the line, so it cannot be pending.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("negative control")
  Rec("HPPIR before    ", GicHighestPending())
  Rec("27 pending      ", GicLinePending(27))
  Rec("27 enabled      ", GicLineEnabled(27))

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  7. HAND THE BOARD BACK
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Say("shutdown")
  GicRelease(GicTickIntId())
  Rec("released enabled", GicLineEnabled(GicTickIntId()))
  GicShutdown()
  Rec("ready after     ", GicReady())

  ; DID THE MACHINE COME BACK THE WAY WE FOUND IT?
  ;
  ; GicInit() disables every line so that whatever the firmware left
  ; enabled cannot fire the instant the CPU interface comes up, and it
  ; SAVES the enables first so that GicShutdown() can put them back.
  ; That is the same contract as fault.pi4's VBAR save and restore and
  ; it exists for the same reason: the monitor is about to load
  ; somebody else's payload and it should not inherit our clean slate.
  ;
  ; This row reads back one line the firmware may have owned. What it
  ; SHOULD say is whatever "27 enabled" said at the top of this
  ; listing, before GicInit() touched anything - if the two disagree,
  ; the save/restore pair is not a pair.
  Rec("27 restored     ", GicLineEnabled(27))
  Rec("never ran       ", pr_neverRan)
  Rec("I masked exit   ", (GicDaif() >> 7) & 1)
  Rec("results         ", pr_n)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("If dist IIDR is $0200143B and cpu IIDR is $0202143B,")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("there is a GIC-400 here and Non-secure EL2 can read it.")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("If witness rc is 1 as well, the boot stub put the")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("interrupts in Group 1 and pi4GicTimer.pi4 is worth")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("running. If it is 0, read the header - that is a")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("firmware finding and no amount of our code fixes it.")
  UartWriteNl()

  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Main()
